  abstract: [
    医学影像分类在疾病筛查与辅助诊断中具有重要价值，但隐私限制以及训练成本高昂等问题严重制约了深度学习模型在临床中的推广部署。数据集蒸馏技术通过将大规模原始训练集压缩为信息密度高的合成数据集，为上述难题的解决提供了新的思路。然而，现有的主流基于扩散模型的数据集蒸馏方法直接迁移至医学图像场景面临三个关键挑战：通用扩散模型缺乏对医学特有纹理和解剖结构的域适配能力。逐类独立聚类的策略忽略了跨类别潜在特征的共享关系，导致决策边界附近的判别信息大量损失。高压缩比下，有限的合成样本难以提供充足的监督信号。

    针对上述挑战，本文提出一种面向医学影像的高效数据集蒸馏方法。以潜在扩散模型为基础，使用低秩适应（Low-Rank Adaptation, LoRA）技术进行参数高效微调，为后续蒸馏合成提供高质量的域适配生成先验。接着在蒸馏阶段，通过扩散模型编码器将所有原始图像映射至低维潜在空间，对所有潜在特征执行全局聚类，以加噪聚类中心为起点执行扩散模型的反向去噪过程，逐步还原为像素空间的蒸馏图像。在训练阶段，先在原始完整数据集上训练教师模型，为蒸馏图像生成软标签，下游分类模型使用蒸馏图像和软标签进行训练。

    与大多数基于扩散模型的蒸馏方法的按类别独立聚类不同，全局聚类允许来自不同类别但特征相近的样本被聚合到同一聚类中心，从而保留类别之间的相似信息以及类别边界地带的模糊信息，而软标签进一步丰富了合成样本的监督信号，帮助下游模型更好的训练。

    在 DermaMNIST（7类皮肤镜图像）、BloodMNIST（8类血细胞图像）和 APTOS‑2019（5类糖尿病视网膜病变图像）三个公开医学数据集上的实验表明，本文方法在不同每类图像数（Image Number Per Class, IPC）设置下均一致优于随机选择与 K‑Means 近邻两种基线。当 IPC=200 时，三个数据集上的分类准确率分别达到 85.29%、98.71% 和 82.24%，与全量数据训练的性能差距分别缩小至约 3.1%、0.3% 和 1.7%。消融实验验证了全局聚类和软标签模块的贡献，二者对最终性能均有显著提升，其中软标签的提升更为明显。与 D⁴M、MGD³、DDQO 等基于扩散模型的蒸馏方法相比，本文方法在收敛速度和最终性能上均表现出明显优势。蒸馏图像具有良好的真实感与类内多样性，训练时间对比表明蒸馏训练可将图像数量减少约 90%，同时大幅缩短训练耗时。上述结果表明，本文方法在保证分类精度的前提下，实现了数据的高效压缩。
  ],
  keywords: ("医学影像分类", "数据集蒸馏", "高效"),
  abstract-en: [
    Medical image classification plays a vital role in disease screening and computer-aided diagnosis, yet its practical deployment is severely hindered by privacy regulations and prohibitive training costs. Dataset distillation offers a promising solution by compressing large-scale training sets into compact, information-dense synthetic sets. However, existing mainstream diffusion-based dataset distillation methods face three key challenges when directly transferred to medical image scenarios: generic diffusion models lack domain adaptation for medical textures and anatomical structures; per-class independent clustering ignores shared cross-class latent features, causing substantial loss of discriminative information near decision boundaries; and limited synthetic samples struggle to provide sufficient supervisory signals under high compression ratios.

    To address these challenges, this thesis proposes an efficient dataset distillation method tailored to medical image classification. Built on a latent diffusion model, we first employ Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) for parameter-efficient fine-tuning, providing a high-quality domain-adapted generative prior for subsequent distillation. In the distillation stage, all original images are encoded into a low-dimensional latent space via the diffusion model's encoder, followed by global clustering on the full set of latent features. Starting from noise-injected cluster centers, the reverse denoising process of the diffusion model progressively reconstructs pixel-space distilled images. In the training stage, a teacher model is pretrained on the full dataset to generate soft labels for the distilled images, and the downstream classification model is trained using both the distilled images and soft labels.

    Unlike per-class independent clustering adopted by most diffusion-based distillation methods, global clustering allows features from different classes that are similar in latent space to be assigned to the same cluster center, thereby preserving inter-class similarity information and ambiguous information near class boundaries. Soft labels further enrich the supervisory signal of the synthetic samples, facilitating better training of the downstream model.

    Extensive experiments on DermaMNIST (7-class dermoscopy), BloodMNIST (8-class blood cell microscopy), and APTOS‑2019 (5-class diabetic retinopathy) demonstrate that the proposed method consistently outperforms random selection and K‑Means neighbor baselines across different IPC (Image Per Class) settings. At IPC=200, the accuracies reach 85.29%, 98.71%, and 82.24%, narrowing the gap to full-data training to approximately 3.1%, 0.3%, and 1.7%, respectively. Ablation studies validate the contributions of both global clustering and soft-label training, with soft-label training showing more pronounced improvements. Compared to existing diffusion-based distillation methods including D⁴M, MGD³, and DDQO, our method achieves faster convergence and superior final performance. The distilled images exhibit good visual fidelity and intra-class diversity, while training time comparisons show an approximately 90% reduction in the number of training images with substantially shorter training time. These results demonstrate that the proposed method achieves efficient data compression without sacrificing classification accuracy.
  ],
  keywords-en: ("Medical image classification", "Dataset distillation", "Efficiency"),
